## Guide

### When Zetian belongs in your draft

Four conditions where she is the correct call:
1. Your team has no other source of global pressure and the enemy jungler rotates freely without consequence.
2. The enemy carries are immobile marksmen or channeling mages who cannot dodge a global stun they cannot see coming.
3. Your frontline is reliable enough that Zetian can stand in the backline undisturbed for the three seconds she needs to complete a full combo.
4. The enemy composition clusters for objectives, making the stun genuinely punishing rather than merely inconvenient.

Three conditions that should make you put her back:
1. Enemy has two or more mobile assassins (Saber, Ling, Fanny, Nolan). One dive she can survive through positioning. Two simultaneous dives from different angles delete her before the passive shield procs.
2. Your team has no crowd control to chain with her ultimate. A 0.8-second stun is a window, not a kill. Without a follow-up knock-up or second CC from a teammate, enemies simply walk away.
3. Your lineup already has a global ultimate (Pharsa, Bane). Two global ultimates on the same team cause coordination friction, not double value.

The disqualifying test: before locking Zetian, name which frontliner will be standing between her and the enemy jungler. If you cannot name that hero, find a different pick.

### The combo order that decides every trade

Zetian's S2 reduces the target's Magic Defense while the spirit is active. That reduction applies immediately. Players who fire S1 first leave a full rotation's worth of amplified damage off the table.

The correct sequence: S2 to place the spirit and slow the enemy, then S1 through the zone. All three waves pass through the slowed target, the third wave's pull keeps them inside the spirit for additional ticks, and every wave lands against reduced magic defense. Reversing the order costs you roughly 15 to 20 percent of total damage on that rotation.

The passive follows the same logic. Phoenix Crown (Spell Vamp stacks) only accumulates when S1 or S2 hits enemy heroes. In a proper combo, one rotation stacks 4 to 5 layers in under two seconds, which means sustained fights become self-healing trades. Standing at range and sending S1 waves into empty space delays those stacks until the fight is already decided.

Phoenix Strike also fires three waves but counts as one cast. You do not need to re-aim after the first wave. Hold the direction and let the third wave's larger hitbox do the work. The small pull on the third wave is enough to drag a retreating enemy back into the S2 spirit zone.

### The first eight minutes

1. **Level 1, take S1.** Phoenix Strike clears the first wave faster than S2, and the level-1 CS advantage compounds through early pressure. Take S2 at level 2 and never put the second point there before level 4.
2. **Poke from outside melee range.** The enemy mid laner will try to walk inside your wave-poke range to force a trade at their preferred distance. Keep moving laterally while casting so the third wave lands behind them, not where they expected.
3. **Do not use Flicker to chase.** The mana-to-damage tradeoff on S1 is favorable, but Zetian without Flicker available is a free kill for any rotating assassin. Flicker is strictly defensive until you have your full CDR core online.

Call for your jungler at level 4, not level 6. By level 4 you have both S1 and S2 at rank 2, the slow is live, and the magic defense reduction is active. A level 4 gank with Zetian's full combo window is substantially stronger than what most mid laners offer at the same timing.

### The item path that unlocks her kit

The 2.1.61 patch ([patch notes](/patch-notes)) pushed S1's mana cost back up from the range set by the December 2.1.40 buff. Zetian is noticeably mana-hungry again at the levels where mid-lane fights start. The first item is Enchanted Talisman, without exception. Its Mana Spring regen sustains repeated S1 rotations, and Magic Mastery raises the CDR cap by 5%. Pair it with Magic Boots and you hit 30% CDR from two items, which drops the ultimate's level 3 cooldown from 70 seconds to 49 seconds. That is the difference between one ult per objective fight and two.

Glowing Wand is the second purchase. S2 deploys a spirit that ticks six times during its active window. Each tick refreshes Glowing Wand's Lifebane debuff, which halves the target's Shield and HP Regen for three seconds. Against healing-heavy compositions (a support Estes, enemy carries itemizing Haas's Claws), this debuff running continuously through your S2 window removes their sustain advantage entirely. It is not primarily a damage item. It is an anti-heal platform that happens to deal damage.

### Teamfight positioning and target priority

The wrong instinct when your tank engages: walk toward the fight to be closer to your targets. Zetian's ultimate reaches every hero on the map from any position. Closing the distance does not increase the stun's value. It halves your survival margin.

Three rules that actually change outcomes:

1. Move to the position farthest from the enemy jungler, not farthest from the enemy frontline. Assassins target the backline. Frontliners target the frontline. The threat you need distance from is the former.
2. Deploy S2 slightly ahead of where enemies are moving, not at their current feet. The spirit charges on recast, which means a well-placed initial drop forces a choice: walk through the zone and absorb ticks, or dodge it and lose their positioning.
3. Use the ultimate to initiate objective dives, not to follow up on an engage already in progress. The stun window that opens during a lord rush on an unsuspecting enemy backline is far more decisive than the same stun landing on enemies who have already started their scatter pattern.

If your support is Estes, you can stand a step closer to the frontline. If your support is Diggie or there is no support, treat your position as if you have no bodyguard at all.

### Itemization: three locked slots, three conversations

The locked slots:
- **Enchanted Talisman** (first item, always, for CDR ceiling and mana regen)
- **Magic Boots** (CDR path, pairs with Talisman) or **Arcane Boots** (penetration path, only if your team already provides a separate CDR source)
- **Glowing Wand** (anti-heal plus sustained magic damage, mandatory before enemies complete their armor)

**Fleeting Time vs. Concentrated Energy** for the third core slot. Fleeting Time's Timestream passive cuts ultimate cooldown by 30% per kill or assist. In an active teamfight where your carry scores three kills, the ult cooldown drops from 49 seconds to about 17 remaining, and every proc after that resets it further. Take it when your team is ahead or when fights resolve quickly. Concentrated Energy's Recharge passive rewards sustained combat: at 6 stacks of 5 Magic Power each, it unlocks a 12% Magic Damage bonus for 3 seconds. Zetian's S1 three-wave sequence plus S2 ticks hits those stacks within a single rotation, making the bonus nearly guaranteed in any real fight. Take it when games are going long and you need output consistency over reset speed.

**Genius Wand vs. Divine Glaive** for the penetration slot. Genius Wand reduces enemy magic defense on successive hits, stacking three times, and combines with S2's own magic defense reduction for compounding debuffs. It outperforms Divine Glaive in mid-game before enemies have bought defensive items. Once enemies complete two or more magic defense items, Divine Glaive's Spellbreaker passive scales harder. Most games: Genius Wand in slot 4, switch to Divine Glaive in the final slot if needed.

**The final slot** is Blood Wings or Holy Crystal. Blood Wings adds a regenerating shield that gives Zetian two independent shield sources (Celestial Robe passive plus Blood Wings Guard passive) and a movement speed burst when the shield breaks. Holy Crystal provides the highest Magic Power ceiling with its 21 to 35 percent scaling at max level. Between the two, Blood Wings wins in any game where an assassin is actively hunting you. Holy Crystal wins when you are ahead and want to close faster.

The weakest common build error is rushing Blood Wings or Holy Crystal before Glowing Wand. The Lifebane reduction during S2 ticks is a team-wide benefit. Skipping it means enemy supports heal through your entire combo in mid-game.

### Mistakes that cost Zetian games

**Pressing the ultimate reactively rather than proactively.** The most common use is: engage breaks out, chaos starts, player presses ult hoping to help. This is the second-worst timing. The strongest timing is seeing the enemy jungler walking toward lord while two of your allies are on the opposite side of the map. That is when a global stun is an equalizer. Landing the same stun on enemies who are already fighting is a minor inconvenience.

**Firing S1 before S2 in a solo trade.** Covered in the combo section, but worth repeating as a mistake because it is the single most common mechanical error on Zetian. S2 is the enabler. S1 is the output. Players who treat S1 as their primary skill and S2 as a follow-up are playing a meaningfully weaker version of the hero.

**Treating Celestial Robe as a reliable escape.** The passive requires an enemy to physically approach Zetian for the knockback and stun to trigger. It does not proc on ranged skills. It does not proc when Saber targets you from across the screen. It will not appear twice in one teamfight. When it procs, reposition immediately. The shield buys one second, not safety.

**Walking into Saber range.** Saber's ultimate suppresses Zetian, deals heavy physical burst, and fires before she can react. The passive shield cannot save her because suppression bypasses normal CC-reaction windows. If Saber is in the enemy draft, the width of your frontline is not a preference. It is the condition under which you exist in the game. If no frontliner stands between you and Saber when he dashes, the game is already losing.

**Skipping Purify against stun-lock compositions.** Flicker is the right default. But against three heroes with hard crowd control, Flicker only fires after the first CC lands if you react fast enough. Purify removes the CC immediately and grants brief control immunity, covering a second stun in the chain. Against compositions built around chaining CC onto the enemy backline, Purify keeps Zetian alive through sequences that Flicker cannot escape.

### Key tips

> [!TIP]
> S2 reduces the target's Magic Defense the moment the spirit appears. Drop it first, then start your S1 sequence immediately. All three waves, including the high-damage third, land against the reduced defense value.

> [!NOTE]
> Zetian's ultimate grants vision of all enemy heroes during its cast. Even if your team is not positioned to fight, using it before a turtle or lord contest to locate enemies is legitimate. The information alone can be worth the cooldown when the timing is short.

> [!WARNING]
> Celestial Robe will not proc twice in one teamfight. Do not stand your ground expecting a second knockback. When it triggers, use the window to create distance and reposition before the next dive.

> [!TIP]
> Fleeting Time stacks fast in a snowball. Three kills or assists in a single teamfight cut a 49-second ult cooldown to about 17 seconds remaining, and a fourth or fifth proc puts it back up before the next objective. Prioritize it over Holy Crystal when your team is converting fights into kills quickly.

## Skills

### Celestial Armament (Passive)

Phoenix Crown: When Zetian hits enemy heroes with Phoenix Strike or Phoenix Descent, she gains 1% Spell Vamp for 5s (up to 10 stacks). Celestial Robe: The power of the Phoenix periodically protects Zetian, knocking back and stunning any enemy hero who approaches her, dealing 90 (+10*Hero Level) (+50% Total Magic Power) Magic Damage. She also gains a 300 (+20*Hero Level) (+50% Total Magic Power) shield for 3s. Cooldown: 120s.

### Phoenix Strike (Skill 1)

Zetian unleashes a Phoenix Wave in the target direction. If it hits an enemy, she automatically casts another wave (up to 3 times). Zetian can cast other skills during this time. The waves deal 150 (+50% Total Magic Power), 150 (+50% Total Magic Power), and 360 (+120% Total Magic Power) Magic Damage, respectively. The 3rd wave has a larger area and pulls enemies toward the cast direction (not considered a control effect). The Spell Vamp ratio against minions is only 65%.

### Phoenix Descent (Skill 2)

Zetian summons a Phoenix Spirit at the target location, dealing 150 (+45% Total Magic Power) Magic Damage and applying a brief 50% slow effect to enemies in the area. The Phoenix Spirit hovers at the location for 3s, dealing 20 (+10% Total Magic Power) Magic Damage every 0.5s and reducing enemies' Magic Defense by 10% while revealing their surrounding area. While the Phoenix Spirit is active, Zetian can use this skill again to command it to charge toward a designated location, dealing 150 (+45% Total Magic Power) Magic Damage and applying a brief 50% slow effect upon arrival.

### Fury of the Phoenix (Ultimate)

Zetian calls upon her divine mandate and the hearts of her people, granting all allied heroes 40% Movement Speed that gradually decays over 3s, while bringing divine retribution upon all enemy heroes, dealing 400 (+100% Total Magic Power) Magic Damage and stunning them for 0.8s. Zetian gains vision of all enemy heroes during this time. Zetian can cast other skills while channeling this skill.

Skill upgrade priority: 3-1-2.

## Combos

- Teamfight Combos (teamfight): In teamfights, use Zetian's Ultimate to boost her team's Movement Speed and launch the global attack, then immediately use her 2nd Skill to slow enemies, making it easier for the Ultimate to land. Follow-up with her 1st Skill for more AoE, and recast the 2nd Skill if enemies try to leave its range.
- Laning Combos (laning): In the laning phase, Use Zetian's 2nd Skill to slow the enemy and reduce their Magic Defense, then use her 1st Skill while moving to reposition and deal sustained damage. If enemies leave the area of the 2nd Skill, use it again to move the Phoenix on top of them.

## Counters to Zetian (pick these against Zetian)

- Valentina (Mage): +2.7 pp edge. Zetian's Ultimate can strike down anywhere on the map, but having such a powerful Ult means a hero like Valentina can also use it against Zetian's team.
- Gloo (Tank): +2.0 pp edge. Gloo's durability and kit give them a strong matchup advantage against Zetian throughout the game.
- Floryn (Support): +1.9 pp edge. Floryn has a statistical edge over Zetian based on ranked matchup data. Pick Floryn to gain an advantage in draft.
- Mathilda (Support/Assassin): +1.8 pp edge. Mathilda has a statistical edge over Zetian based on ranked matchup data. Pick Mathilda to gain an advantage in draft.
- Diggie (Support): +1.7 pp edge. Diggie has a statistical edge over Zetian based on ranked matchup data. Pick Diggie to gain an advantage in draft.

